223 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 91 0 R
/T (The trigger was an energy shock meeting resilient demand. Disruption to oil flows through the Strait of Hormuz left ICE Brent near US$105 a barrel when the IEA wrote its September report, up US$21 since the start of August. On its own, an oil shock raises headline inflation but also squeezes household spending, which tends to cool the economy. This time demand held up: the US flash composite PMI rose from 56.0 in August to 58.4 in September, the fastest growth in over five years. With demand that strong, companies can pass higher fuel and input costs on to customers, raising the risk that the energy shock spreads into broader and more persistent inflation.)
/E (The trigger was an energy shock meeting resilient demand. Disruption to oil flows through the Strait of Hormuz left ICE Brent near US$105 a barrel when the IEA wrote its September report, up US$21 since the start of August. On its own, an oil shock raises headline inflation but also squeezes household spending, which tends to cool the economy. This time demand held up: the US flash composite PMI rose from 56.0 in August to 58.4 in September, the fastest growth in over five years. With demand that strong, companies can pass higher fuel and input costs on to customers, raising the risk that the energy shock spreads into broader and more persistent inflation.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 91 0 R
/T (Central banks responded by tightening. The Fed raised its target range by 25bps to 3.75% to 4.00% on 16 September, its first hike since 2023. The ECB raised its three key rates by 25bps on 10 September, taking the deposit rate to 2.50%. Its staff projections matter more than the hike itself: inflation of 3.0% in 2026, 2.5% in 2027 and 2.1% in 2028 keeps the forecast above the 2% target in every year, which signals that rates are unlikely to fall quickly.)
/E (Central banks responded by tightening. The Fed raised its target range by 25bps to 3.75% to 4.00% on 16 September, its first hike since 2023. The ECB raised its three key rates by 25bps on 10 September, taking the deposit rate to 2.50%. Its staff projections matter more than the hike itself: inflation of 3.0% in 2026, 2.5% in 2027 and 2.1% in 2028 keeps the forecast above the 2% target in every year, which signals that rates are unlikely to fall quickly.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 91 0 R
/T (For issuers, the key change is that the market stopped pricing a path to progressively cheaper money. Before September, borrowers could reasonably expect funding costs to drift lower over time. Now a borrower planning a 10y financing prices off a higher expected path for short rates, plus extra compensation for uncertainty. Waiting for a cheaper window is no longer a safe default, and the cost of locking in long-term funding has risen for every borrower, whatever its credit quality.)
/E (For issuers, the key change is that the market stopped pricing a path to progressively cheaper money. Before September, borrowers could reasonably expect funding costs to drift lower over time. Now a borrower planning a 10y financing prices off a higher expected path for short rates, plus extra compensation for uncertainty. Waiting for a cheaper window is no longer a safe default, and the cost of locking in long-term funding has risen for every borrower, whatever its credit quality.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
