/Title (The trigger was an energy shock meeting resilient demand. Disruption to oil flows through the Strait of Hormuz left ICE Brent near US$105 a barrel when the IEA wrote its September report, up US$21 since the start of August. On its own, an oil shock raises headline inflation but also squeezes household spending, which tends to cool the economy. This time demand held up: the US flash composite PMI rose from 56.0 in August to 58.4 in September, the fastest growth in over five years. With demand that strong, companies can pass higher fuel and input costs on to customers, raising the risk that the energy shock spreads into broader and more persistent inflation.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2811 0 R
/Next 2855 0 R
>>
endobj
