/Title (Central banks responded by tightening. The Fed raised its target range by 25bps to 3.75% to 4.00% on 16 September, its first hike since 2023. The ECB raised its three key rates by 25bps on 10 September, taking the deposit rate to 2.50%. Its staff projections matter more than the hike itself: inflation of 3.0% in 2026, 2.5% in 2027 and 2.1% in 2028 keeps the forecast above the 2% target in every year, which signals that rates are unlikely to fall quickly.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2811 0 R
/Prev 2831 0 R
/Next 2856 0 R
>>
endobj
2856 0 obj
<<
/Title (For issuers, the key change is that the market stopped pricing a path to progressively cheaper money. Before September, borrowers could reasonably expect funding costs to drift lower over time. Now a borrower planning a 10y financing prices off a higher expected path for short rates, plus extra compensation for uncertainty. Waiting for a cheaper window is no longer a safe default, and the cost of locking in long-term funding has risen for every borrower, whatever its credit quality.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2811 0 R
/Prev 2855 0 R
/Next 2832 0 R
>>
endobj
