2908 0 obj
<<
/Title (Central banks set the overnight rate, but investors price the years that follow. A 10y yield reflects the expected average path of short-term rates over the next decade, plus extra compensation for holding duration. That compensation covers the risk that inflation runs hotter than expected, that heavy government borrowing pushes yields higher, and that an investor may need to sell before maturity in a weaker market. When any of those risks rises, long yields can climb even if the central bank does nothing.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2891 0 R
/Next 2926 0 R
>>
endobj
2909 0 obj
<<
/Title (These charts cannot isolate the term premium on their own, because the move mixes changing rate expectations with changing compensation for risk. What they do show is that the repricing was broad and persistent. Even after partial rallies, benchmark yields stayed well above their earlier levels across the US, UK, France, Germany and Japan. That is now the starting point at which governments and companies have to finance heavy borrowing, before any credit spread is added on top.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2891 0 R
/Prev 2926 0 R
>>
endobj
